 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Welcome to Episode Thirteen of Season Three of Kukhnya. After a wild night out, Max wakes up in Katya's bed in complete shock, while Dmitry Nagiyev wakes up on a narrow sofa at Vika's apartment. Listen to the full conversation first, and then we will unpack every line together.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Мы старательно собираем пазлы.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Хочется составить картинку своего счастья.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Не всегда пазлы складываются правильно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Нифига себе, где это я?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Доброе утро, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Доброе утро, Катя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Ну, кому как сегодня.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Знал бы я про свою судьбу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Судьба повернулась ко мне спиной.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Конечно, диван бы купил раскладной.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Как спалось сегодня ночью?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Неплохо, спасибо тебе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты мои джинсы не видела?",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Мы старательно собираем пазлы.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We carefully put jigsaw puzzles together.' Max anchors the line on 'we', while 'jigsaw puzzles' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Хочется составить картинку своего счастья.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We want to put together a picture of our happiness.' The meaning hinges on 'want' working together with 'happiness' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Не всегда пазлы складываются правильно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Puzzles do not always come together the right way.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'not' and finishing on 'correctly'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Нифига себе, где это я?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Holy cow, where am I?' Max brings out the contrast between 'holy cow' and 'to oneself' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Доброе утро, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good morning, Max.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'good' and finishing on 'Max'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Доброе утро, Катя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good morning, Katya.' Notice how Russian expresses 'good' and 'Katya' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Dmitry Nagiyev answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Ну, кому как сегодня.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, that depends on who you ask today.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'come on' before landing on 'tonight'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Знал бы я про свою судьбу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'If only I had known about my fate.' The grammar packs 'knew' and 'destiny' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Судьба повернулась ко мне спиной.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Fate turned its back on me.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'fate' and finishing on 'back'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Конечно, диван бы купил раскладной.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Of course, I would have bought a fold-out sofa.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'of course' before landing on 'fold-out'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nagiyev",
   "t": "Как спалось сегодня ночью?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'How did you sleep last night?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'how' and finishing on 'at night'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Неплохо, спасибо тебе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Not bad, thank you.' Max addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'to you'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты мои джинсы не видела?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Have you seen my jeans?' Max anchors the line on 'you', while 'saw' completes the thought. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
